  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1235 AM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nThe main concern for the near term part of the forecast revolves \naround rain chances this weekend. An upper low over northern Florida \nwill shift northward into the upstate South Carolina region through \nthe day, before being kicked off to the northeast on Sunday by a \nshortwave moving east from Mississippi valley. Models had been \nshowing dry conditions across our forecast area the last few days \nbut have trended towards bringing in some light rain from the \nsoutheast as the upper low shifts through the Carolinas. Regional \nradar imagery shows some measurable precipitation generally near the \nI-95 corridor in Georgia and South Carolina this evening. Meanwhile \ndownstream over the southern Appalachians, a considerable amount of \ndry air remains in place. The Cove Mountain NPS air quality \nmonitoring station was still showing temperatures in the mid 50s \nwith an RH of 14 percent at 11pm. The NBM westward extent of rain \nchances for this afternoon through early Sun morning didn't look too \nbad, but I think the onset of rain was too quick so I toned that \ndown a bit. On a related note, considering the dry air in place and \nthe delaying of rainfall onset, I also lowered dewpoints once again \ntoday this afternoon. As a result, I once again have some low \nafternoon RH values in the forecast (fairly widespread 25-35 percent \nvalues) this afternoon. They'll be better than yesterday afternoon, \nbut still low. Lastly, as the upper low lifts into the Carolinas \ntoday, easterly H85 flow of 15-20 kt will spread across the southern \nAppalachians. The setup isn't going to produce downslope winds, but \nthe mountains could be a bit breezy this afternoon. Winds won't be \nstrong enough for any wind headlines, but fire weather concerns may \nbe a bit elevated until the rainfall arrives. Not sure it rises to \nhigh enough concern to warrant any fire weather headlines, but will \nhighlight it in the FWF.\n\nAs mentioned, the upper low gets kicked to the northeast on Sunday \nby a cold front and associated upper trough moving in from the \nMississippi valley. That cold front will be moisture starved and \nfeature waning upper support, so showers should be fairly sparse \nwith the frontal passage Sunday night into Monday. \n\nRainfall this weekend (both systems) will largely be tied to the \nmountains, and will be light at that. Totals through monday morning \nwill mostly fall in the 0.10 to 0.25 inch range, with the best \nchances to exceed that being over our NC counties.\n\nCooler temps and dry weather are expected Monday through Tuesday \nbehind the frontal passage with temperatures back down closer to \nseasonal normals. Our next chance of precip comes Wed/Thu as an \nupper trough approaches from the northwest and a system in the Gulf \napproaches from the south.",
